                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <div class="fail-tag critical">FAILURE MODE 1</div>
                            <div class="method-label">Text-Based Statistical Tests</div>
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            MMD, hypothesis tests on text output distributions; compare API vs. reference
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Quantized models produce near-identical output distributions — effect size too small to reliably detect
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Impractically large query budgets required; fails entirely against subtle substitutions (e.g., FP8 variants within error bars)
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <div class="fail-tag critical">FAILURE MODE 2</div>
                            <div class="method-label">Log-Probability Fingerprinting</div>
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Compare token-level log-probs from local reference model vs. API responses
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Production nondeterminism — same weights produce different log-probs across framework versions and GPU hardware
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Cannot distinguish legitimate infrastructure change (A100→H100, vLLM upgrade) from actual model substitution
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <div class="fail-tag high">FAILURE MODE 3</div>
                            <div class="method-label">Adversarial Countermeasures</div>
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Auditor sends probe queries; provider cannot anticipate which queries are audits
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Provider can detect known benchmark prompts and route them to genuine model while substituting on regular traffic
                        </td>
                        <td style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
                            Randomized substitution at low <em>p</em> defeats all statistical tests — mixed distribution converges to specified model as <em>p → 0</em>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="verdict-box reveal stagger-4">
            <div class="verdict-icon">✗</div>
            <div class="verdict-text">
                <strong>Verdict:</strong> All three software-only approaches share a common failure: the adversary (provider) controls the execution environment. Without hardware-level attestation, no software test can provide provable guarantees.
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>
    <div class="slide-number">07 / 09</div>
</section>

